Q: PHP preg_replace multiple replacements A: <p>Simple solution using <code>preg_match</code> and <code>str_replace</code> functions:</p>
<pre><code>$testString = &quot;This is text I don't care about | 7.3 seconds ago&quot;;
$timeUnitsmap = [&quot;secs&quot; =&gt; &quot;seconds&quot;, &quot;mins&quot; =&gt; &quot;minutes&quot;, &quot;hrs&quot; =&gt; &quot;hours&quot;];

preg_match(&quot;/\b[0-9.]+? \w+?\b/&quot;, $testString, $matches);
$testString = str_replace(array_values($timeUnitsmap), array_keys($timeUnitsmap), $matches[0]);

print_r($testString);  // &quot;7.3 secs&quot;
</code></pre>
<p>Or using <code>preg_replace_callback</code> and <code>array_search</code> functions:</p>
<pre><code>...
$testString = preg_replace_callback([&quot;/.*?\b([0-9.]+? )(\w+?)\b.*/&quot;], function($m) use($timeUnitsmap){
    return $m[1]. array_search($m[2], $timeUnitsmap);
}, $testString);
</code></pre>

Q: C++ reflection - compile time index access - but not with a macro A: <p>Summing up the <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/79878956/2085626">answer by @HolyBlackCat</a> and <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/79878950/c-reflection-compile-time-index-access-but-not-with-a-macro/79878956#comment140963434_79878956">comment by @Red.Wave</a>, these two options work nicely:</p>
<p><strong>Option 1</strong>, as suggested by <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/users/2752075/holyblackcat">@HolyBlackCat</a>:</p>
<pre><code>template&lt;size_t from, size_t to, typename F&gt;
void compile_time_index_access(size_t index, F&amp;&amp; f) {
    template for(constexpr size_t i : std::ranges::views::iota(from, to)) {
        if(i == index) {
            f.template operator()&lt;i&gt;();
            break;
        }
    }
}

// Caller:
std::any anys[] = {42, char(42)};
static constexpr std::array meta_types = {^^int, ^^char};
size_t index = 1;
compile_time_index_access&lt;0, 2&gt;(index,
  [&amp;anys]&lt;size_t i&gt;() {
    cout &lt;&lt; std::any_cast&lt;typename [:meta_types[i]:]&gt;(anys[i]) &lt;&lt; endl;
  }
);
</code></pre>
<p>Code: <a href="https://reflection.godbolt.org/z/vf7oW9ovz" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://reflection.godbolt.org/z/vf7oW9ovz</a></p>
<hr />
<p><strong>Option 2</strong>, a small change in how we pass <em>i</em>, as suggested by <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/users/6385640/red-wave">@Red.Wave</a> and using the suggestion by @Artyr in his comment below:</p>
<pre><code>template&lt;auto i&gt;
constexpr auto as_constexpr = std::integral_constant&lt;decltype(i), i&gt;{};

template&lt;size_t from, size_t to, typename F&gt;
void compile_time_index_access(size_t index, F&amp;&amp; f) {
    template for(constexpr size_t i : std::ranges::views::iota(from, to)) {
        if(i == index) {
            f(as_constexpr&lt;i&gt;);
            break;
        }
    }
}

// Caller:
std::any anys[] = {42, char(42)};
static constexpr std::array meta_types = {^^int, ^^char};
size_t index = 1;
compile_time_index_access&lt;0, 2&gt;(index,
  [&amp;anys]&lt;size_t i&gt;(auto i) {
    cout &lt;&lt; std::any_cast&lt;typename [:meta_types[i]:]&gt;(anys[i]) &lt;&lt; endl;
  }
);
</code></pre>
<p>Code: <a href="https://reflection.godbolt.org/z/ssnv6ber5" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://reflection.godbolt.org/z/ssnv6ber5</a></p>
<hr />
<p>The main ideas in both:</p>
<ol>
<li><p>Constant values, declared in consteval context, are not considered consteval when passed to a function as arguments, thus need to pass them as a template argument. (I should have known that based on <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/79667841/2085626">this SO answer</a>... :).</p>
</li>
<li><p>A static constexpr is captured automatically, as constexpr, by a lambda in a nested scope.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Calling a template function passed as a template argument, can be done either with directly providing the template argument, as in: <code>f.template operator()&lt;i&gt;();</code> or by using an argument that helps the compiler deduce the template argument, using  <code>std::integral_constant&lt;size_t, i&gt;</code> (or a user-defined shorter alias of it) with a proper adaptation in the lambda as seen in option 2 above.</p>
</li>
</ol>

Q: Clear text using Console.Clear A: <p>You can find all the properties and methods of the <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.console" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Console here</a>.</p>
<p>There is a <code>SetCursorPosition(Int32, Int32)</code> method allowing you to write text at a specific place.</p>
<p>You could create your own method accepting coordinates and a text instead of relying on <code>Console.WriteLine</code> which allows you to write text only in sequence:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>static void WriteAt(int left, int top, string text)
{
    Console.SetCursorPosition(left, top);
    Console.Write(text);
}
</code></pre>
<p>Like this you have more control.</p>

Q: UI Freezing when passing prop function from parent to child component A: <p>Your issue is caused by parent rerendering and in turn, child component rerenders too. And those rerenders happen in your code because <code>message</code> state updates from children happen on every click.</p>
<p>In order to prevent rerendering children component, you need to memoize the component, as it updates parent state, but child component does not change due to that. So we can use <code>memo</code>.</p>
<p>But now we need to also &quot;stabilize&quot; the prop passed to children component. This can be done with <code>useCallback</code> hook.</p>
<p>In order to fix this you should:</p>
<ul>
<li>Memoize the callback in the parent using <code>useCallback</code></li>
<li>Memoize the child component using <code>memo</code></li>
</ul>
<p>Below is simplified setup of your components:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const Support = () =&gt; {
  const [message, setMessage] = useState(null);

  const handleChildData = useCallback((data) =&gt; {
    setMessage(data);
  }, []);

  return (
    &lt;&gt;
      &lt;h1&gt;{message} : Data from Child&lt;/h1&gt;
      &lt;GridData handleChildData={handleChildData} /&gt;
    &lt;/&gt;
  );
};

const GridData = React.memo(({ handleChildData }) =&gt; {
  const table = useMaterialReactTable(
    useMemo(() =&gt; ({
      data,
      columns,
      muiTableBodyRowProps: ({ row }) =&gt; ({
        onClick: () =&gt; handleChildData(row.original.name),
        sx: { cursor: 'pointer' },
      }),
    }), [data, columns, handleChildData])
  );

  return &lt;MaterialReactTable table={table} /&gt;;
});
</code></pre>
<p><strong>BONUS</strong></p>
<p>Setters returned from <code>useState</code> hook are stable, so you can pass <code>setMesage</code> function and omit entirely</p>
<pre><code>const handleChildData = useCallback((data) =&gt; {
    setMessage(data);
}, []);
</code></pre>
<p>and then pass <code>setMessage</code> directly</p>
<pre><code>&lt;GridData handleChildData={setMessage} /&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>It will keep working the same :)</p>
